Inhomogeneous direct correlation function from MD via OZ inversion (proof of concept)
=====================================================================================

These examples drive "compute structure/factor/2d", which measures the bin-resolved
(planar) static structure factor S_ij(k_parallel) of a fluid whose density varies
along z.  Time-averaged with fix ave/time, S_ij(k) is inverted (in Python) through the
inhomogeneous Ornstein-Zernike equation to obtain the inhomogeneous direct correlation
function C(z1,z2,s).  For a liquid-vapor slab this yields the Triezenberg-Zwanzig (TZ)
inhomogeneous Helmholtz energy density psi_IH(z) and surface tension directly from MD,
instead of the local-density + Percus-Yevick approximation.

Requirements
------------
- LAMMPS built with the EXTRA-COMPUTE package.
- Python 3 with NumPy (matplotlib optional, for --plot).
- The simulation box must be square in x and y (Lx == Ly); the compute aborts otherwise.

compute structure/factor/2d syntax
----------------------------------
    compute ID group-ID structure/factor/2d kmax nbins

    kmax  = maximum reciprocal-lattice index (circular cutoff radius in k-space)
    nbins = number of z-bins (interface-normal direction)

Output: a global array with columns [q=|k_parallel|, ibin, jbin, S_ij, density].
The per-bin density rho_i is stored on the q==0 rows (column 5).  Values are
instantaneous and unnormalized -- divide by area and time-average externally.

Workflow
--------
1. Bulk validation:
       lmp -in in.bulk_lj
       python3 oz_invert.py --mode bulk --sf-file sf_bulk.out --nbins 8 \
               --lx <Lx> --lz <Lz> --temp 0.90 --rdf-file rdf_bulk.out
   Checks that the inverted C_ij is translationally invariant in z and that its
   z-Fourier transform reproduces the bulk c(k) ~ (1 - 1/S(q))/rho.

2. Liquid-vapor slab:
       lmp -in in.slab_lj
       python3 oz_invert.py --mode slab --sf-file sf_slab.out --nbins 40 \
               --lx <Lx> --lz <Lz> --temp 0.80
   Produces psi_IH(z) and the TZ surface tension; compare gamma to the mechanical
   value in gamma_slab.out.

Get Lx and Lz from the LAMMPS log (printed box bounds) or thermo output.

3. Field-coupling (Kirkwood) EOS route (field_coupling.py):
   An independent, pressure-tensor-free route to the homogeneous EOS and the
   interfacial free energy, from a LADDER of CPP runs at increasing field
   amplitude (U_ext = A cos(2 pi z/Lz), A = dUmax/2).
       python3 field_coupling.py --ladder ladder.csv --lz <Lz> --temp <T> \
               [--kappa <c>] [--deg <n>]
   where ladder.csv lists "dumax,density_file" per line.  It returns:
     * mu0(rho), P0(rho) (the van der Waals loop below Tc) from the LOCAL
       Euler-Lagrange condition mu0(rho(z)) + mu_IH(z) + U_ext(z) = mu_tot.  The
       gradient correction mu_IH = sum_g b_g d^g rho/dz^g (even g) is FIT from the
       ladder (--grad-orders, default 2,4) rather than assuming the second-order
       square-gradient closure b_2 rho'' (= -kappa rho'', the VdW form that fails
       at sharp interfaces) -- a ladder samples each rho at different gradients, so
       the b_g are identifiable.  Pointwise in rho(z), well conditioned.
     * INT psi_IH dz and gamma = 2 INT psi_IH from the exact Hellmann-Feynman
       coupling integral, cross-checked against the square-gradient (kappa/2) INT
       rho'^2 dz.
   Self-test: python3 field_coupling.py --selftest  (recovers a known EOS + kappa
   from EL-solved profiles to ~1e-14 / few %).  Validated on the supercritical
   CPP-1 snapshot (single field strength, A=2.32): the direct relation holds to
   2.4% rms, and with the OZ-route gradient kappa~4 the recovered mu0(rho) lands
   on the LJ EOS to 0.038 rms over rho=0.04-0.75.  NOTE: a single field strength
   gives mu0(rho) (with kappa supplied) but not the coupling integral (gamma needs
   the ladder), and cannot separate kappa from mu_ex on its own.

Notes / caveats
---------------
- The OZ inversion is ill-conditioned in low-density (vapor) bins; oz_invert.py drops
  bins below --rho-min and reports the condition number of (I + D h).
- ROBUSTNESS: the s^3-weighted TZ second moment INT ds s^3 c is dominated by the
  long-range attractive tail of c, where c(r) -> -beta u(r) (mean field / RPA).  The
  small-k slope of the inverted c(k) estimates this tail poorly (the smallest
  k = 2*pi/Lx is not in the k^2 regime for Lx ~ 13 sigma) and noisily, giving a ~2x
  LOW bias in gamma_TZ.  The --tail-rsplit option (default 1.5 sigma, requires scipy)
  takes that tail ANALYTICALLY from -beta u(r) and fits only the smooth short-range
  residual; this removes the bias and the noise sensitivity, so the method works for
  small/short runs.  On a 100k-step Lx=13 sigma snapshot it brings gamma_TZ from 0.38
  to 0.62 (mechanical full-cell 0.72) and psi_IH onto the published curve.  Validate
  gamma_TZ against the full-cell mechanical gamma = Lz*<Pzz-(Pxx+Pyy)/2>.
- The s^3 weighting in the TZ second moment emphasizes the large-s tail of C(s); a
  -beta*u(s) tail correction beyond s = L/2 is a natural refinement (not yet applied).
- This is a proof of concept: parameters (run length, kmax, nbins, box size) are modest
  and should be converged for production use.
